static const TimelineStep kTl[] = {
    {"about 400 BC", "Democritus and Leucippus, Greece", "Atoms as an idea", "If you keep cutting something in half, they said, you must reach a piece that cannot be cut: an <i>atomos</i> (\"uncuttable\"). It was philosophy, not an experiment. Nobody could test it for more than 2,000 years.",
     "<circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"16\" fill=\"#9aa5b8\"/>",
     "", {}},
    {"1803", "John Dalton, England", "Solid balls", "Chemistry gave the first evidence. Each element is made of its own kind of atom, and atoms join in fixed ratios (water is always 2 hydrogen to 1 oxygen). Atoms were pictured as tiny solid balls.",
     "<defs><radialGradient id=\"dg\" cx=\".35\" cy=\".3\"><stop offset=\"0\" stop-color=\"#cfd8ea\"/><stop offset=\"1\" stop-color=\"#5c6b88\"/></radialGradient></defs><circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"17\" fill=\"url(#dg)\"/>",
     "", {}},
    {"1869", "Dmitri Mendeleev, Russia", "The periodic table", "Mendeleev lined up the known elements by weight and spotted a repeating pattern. He left gaps for elements nobody had found yet and predicted what they would be like. Gallium and germanium were discovered later and matched his predictions closely.",
     "<rect x=\"8\" y=\"10\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#8fb0e0\"/><rect x=\"20\" y=\"10\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#e0a070\"/><rect x=\"32\" y=\"10\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#8fd0a0\"/><rect x=\"8\" y=\"22\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#e0a070\"/><rect x=\"20\" y=\"22\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#8fd0a0\"/><rect x=\"32\" y=\"22\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#7a86a0\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\"/><rect x=\"8\" y=\"34\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#8fd0a0\"/><rect x=\"20\" y=\"34\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#7a86a0\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\"/><rect x=\"32\" y=\"34\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#8fb0e0\"/>",
     "", {}},
    {"1896–1898", "Henri Becquerel in France; Marie and Pierre Curie, Poland and France", "Radioactivity", "Becquerel found that uranium gives off invisible rays all by itself. The Curies discovered two new radioactive elements, polonium and radium, and Marie gave the effect its name. Atoms were not unchangeable after all: some of them slowly turn into other elements.",
     "<circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#7ad04a\"/><circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"12\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#7ad04a\" opacity=\".7\"/><circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"20\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#7ad04a\" opacity=\".4\"/>",
     "Look at radium-226 in the lab", {{"Z", "88"}, {"N", "138"}}},
    {"1897", "J. J. Thomson, England", "The electron, and the \"plum pudding\"", "Thomson found a particle about 1,800 times lighter than hydrogen: the electron. So atoms have parts! His model had negative electrons scattered inside a positive blob, like raisins in a pudding.",
     "<circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"20\" fill=\"#f0c8c0\"/><circle cx=\"20\" cy=\"22\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2a5db0\"/><circle cx=\"34\" cy=\"20\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2a5db0\"/><circle cx=\"26\" cy=\"34\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2a5db0\"/><circle cx=\"37\" cy=\"33\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2a5db0\"/><circle cx=\"19\" cy=\"35\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2a5db0\"/>",
     "", {}},
    {"1905–1909", "Albert Einstein, then Jean Perrin, France", "Proof that atoms exist", "Even in 1900 many famous scientists doubted that atoms were real. Einstein worked out that the jittery dance of tiny grains in water must come from invisible molecules bumping them. Perrin measured the jitter, and the numbers matched. The argument was over.",
     "<path d=\"M8 40 L16 30 L22 38 L30 20 L36 34 L42 16 L48 28\" stroke=\"#4a8ad2\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\"/><circle cx=\"30\" cy=\"20\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#4a8ad2\"/>",
     "", {}},
    {"1909–1911", "Ernest Rutherford (with Geiger and Marsden), New Zealand and England", "The nucleus", "They fired particles at gold foil. Most went straight through, but about 1 in 8,000 bounced almost straight back. Rutherford: \"as if you fired a shell at tissue paper and it came back.\" The positive charge must sit in a tiny, dense nucleus, and the rest of the atom is empty.",
     "<circle cx=\"34\" cy=\"28\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#d24a4a\"/><path d=\"M4 18 L30 26\" stroke=\"#7a86a0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path d=\"M4 30 L48 30\" stroke=\"#7a86a0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path d=\"M4 40 L32 31 L18 8\" stroke=\"#c85a2a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/>",
     "See the tiny nucleus in the lab", {{"zoom", "3"}}},
    {"1913", "Niels Bohr, Denmark", "Electrons on fixed orbits", "Electrons circle the nucleus like planets, but only on certain allowed orbits. When one jumps down to a lower orbit, it gives out a photon of one exact colour. This explained the colours in hydrogen light for the first time.",
     "<circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#d24a4a\"/><circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"10\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#7a86a0\"/><circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"19\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#7a86a0\"/><circle cx=\"38\" cy=\"28\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2a5db0\"/>",
     "Watch Bohr's orbit and photon jumps", {{"view", "Orbit"}, {"zoom", "0"}}},
    {"1913", "Henry Moseley, England", "Atomic number = protons", "Moseley measured the X-rays each element gives off and found a whole number for each one. That number is the number of protons in its nucleus. It put the periodic table in its true order and showed exactly which elements were still missing. He was killed in World War I at the age of 27.",
     "<text x=\"28\" y=\"34\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"24\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#d24a4a\" font-family=\"system-ui,sans-serif\">Z</text><circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"20\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#7a86a0\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 3\"/>",
     "", {}},
    {"1919", "Ernest Rutherford, England", "The proton, and the first changed atom", "Rutherford shot fast particles into nitrogen gas and hydrogen nuclei came flying out. So the hydrogen nucleus, the proton, sits inside other atoms. It was also the first time anyone changed one element into another on purpose: nitrogen into oxygen.",
     "<circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#d24a4a\"/><text x=\"28\" y=\"33\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#fff\" font-family=\"system-ui,sans-serif\">+</text>",
     "Look at a single proton", {{"Z", "1"}, {"N", "0"}}},
    {"1924–1927", "Louis de Broglie, France; Davisson and Germer, USA", "Matter is a wave too", "de Broglie asked: if light can act like a particle, can a particle act like a wave? Three years later, electrons fired at a crystal made a wave pattern. Electrons really do behave like waves, which is what makes the electron cloud possible.",
     "<path d=\"M6 28 Q13 10 20 28 T34 28 T48 28\" stroke=\"#2a5db0\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\"/>",
