    {"1925", "Wolfgang Pauli, Austria", "Why electrons form shells", "Pauli’s rule: no two electrons can be in exactly the same state. So electrons cannot all crowd into the lowest level. They fill shells one after another, and that is what creates the rows of the periodic table, and the different chemistry of each element. The lab draws only one electron, so it cannot show a full shell like this: the button opens a neon nucleus with a single electron (Ne⁹⁺).",
     "<circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#d24a4a\"/><circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"10\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#7a86a0\"/><circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"18\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#7a86a0\"/><circle cx=\"38\" cy=\"28\" r=\"2.4\" fill=\"#2a5db0\"/><circle cx=\"18\" cy=\"28\" r=\"2.4\" fill=\"#2a5db0\"/><circle cx=\"46\" cy=\"28\" r=\"2.4\" fill=\"#2a5db0\"/><circle cx=\"10\" cy=\"28\" r=\"2.4\" fill=\"#2a5db0\"/><circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"10\" r=\"2.4\" fill=\"#2a5db0\"/>",
     "Open a neon nucleus with one electron", {{"Z", "10"}, {"N", "10"}, {"zoom", "0"}}},
    {"1925–1926", "Heisenberg, Schrödinger and Born, Germany and Austria", "Electron clouds", "Electrons behave like waves. You cannot say where the electron is, only where it is <i>likely</i> to be. The orbit becomes a cloud of probability. This model predicts almost everything about atoms, and it is the one the lab uses for the Cloud views.",
     "<defs><radialGradient id=\"cg\"><stop offset=\"0\" stop-color=\"#2a5db0\" stop-opacity=\".9\"/><stop offset=\"1\" stop-color=\"#2a5db0\" stop-opacity=\"0\"/></radialGradient></defs><circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"22\" fill=\"url(#cg)\"/><circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#d24a4a\"/>",
