    {"1938–1939", "Otto Hahn and Fritz Strassmann, Germany; Lise Meitner and Otto Frisch, Austria and Sweden", "Splitting the nucleus", "Hahn and Strassmann hit uranium with neutrons and found barium, an element much lighter than uranium. Meitner and Frisch explained it: the nucleus had split in two, releasing a huge amount of energy, exactly as E = mc² says. This is nuclear fission, the source of nuclear power.",
     "<circle cx=\"20\" cy=\"28\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#d27a2a\"/><circle cx=\"38\" cy=\"28\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#d27a2a\"/><path d=\"M28 8 L28 18 M28 38 L28 48\" stroke=\"#e0b020\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>",
     "Look at uranium-235", {{"Z", "92"}, {"N", "143"}, {"zoom", "0"}}},
    {"1964–1969", "Gell-Mann and Zweig proposed it; experiments at SLAC (USA) confirmed it", "Quarks", "Protons and neutrons are not solid either. Each is made of three quarks, held together by the strong force and gluons. Electrons fired at protons bounced as if hitting three tiny points inside. Quarks have never been seen alone.",
     "<circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"20\" r=\"7\" fill=\"#e04a4a\"/><circle cx=\"19\" cy=\"35\" r=\"7\" fill=\"#3aa860\"/><circle cx=\"37\" cy=\"35\" r=\"7\" fill=\"#3a6ad8\"/>",
